1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:01,520
Paul Francis used that term.

2
00:00:01,760 --> 00:00:05,360
He says this is an exciting technology but also a fearsome technology.

3
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:09,600
So it's okay to have that fear but that fear sometimes comes from ignorance.

4
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:19,760
So if we can defeat ignorance then we have a chance to move from something that could be potentially harmful to something that could be really harnessed for good.

5
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:30,880
Edmundo and Edmund, it's a real pleasure to have the opportunity to speak with both of you today.

6
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:36,160
Just kind of starting out, I know that both of you come from strong ministry backgrounds.

7
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:39,840
Edmund, I know that you worked in parish ministry for quite a while.

8
00:00:40,320 --> 00:00:44,640
Edmundo, I know on the diocesan level in Detroit, you worked in communications.

9
00:00:45,280 --> 00:00:53,520
If you guys could share for our audience who might not know so much about you guys, how did those experiences shape how you see technology's role in the church today?

10
00:00:53,920 --> 00:00:55,680
And maybe Edmundo, we can start with you.

11
00:00:57,040 --> 00:00:58,400
Well, thank you so much, Benjamin.

12
00:00:58,480 --> 00:01:01,120
I'm so happy to be here and with Edmund as well..

13
00:01:02,160 --> 00:01:05,520
He's a close collaborator and a friend and we're happy to be here.

14
00:01:06,320 --> 00:01:07,360
Yeah, that's a great question.

15
00:01:07,920 --> 00:01:19,840
So I was in college during the digital revolution, the first one, so I got to witness firsthand the excitement around technologies.

16
00:01:21,040 --> 00:01:23,200
We would call them emerging technologies at that time.

17
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:32,480
And in fact, I was studying economics and I switched to communications because of the excitement that I had with these new technologies.

18
00:01:32,720 --> 00:01:41,440
And it just sounds funny now when you say new technologies, surging technologies, and you're referring to the internet and email, but it was it was like mind blowing.

19
00:01:41,520 --> 00:01:46,960
It was it was the time where, yeah, just things were changing and very, very quickly.

20
00:01:48,960 --> 00:01:52,640
So, at that time I was seventeen years old, I switched I had a powerful encounter with the Lord.

21
00:01:52,960 --> 00:01:57,040
I went to this retreat and had a very, very powerful encounter with the Lord.

22
00:01:58,080 --> 00:02:04,560
And I felt called to be at the intersection of technology and media and evangelization from the very very beginning.

23
00:02:04,800 --> 00:02:09,200
So that's been always part of who I am and what I do with my career.

24
00:02:09,680 --> 00:02:13,760
And I've been very blessed to be able to serve the Lord in that intersection.

25
00:02:15,040 --> 00:02:17,120
So at that time, again, I was young.

26
00:02:17,840 --> 00:02:19,280
I was looking at all these technologies.

27
00:02:19,840 --> 00:02:34,400
And one of the things that was always so encouraging to me was the approach of the church that it was never, it was always providing wisdom on how to navigate this changing world, but it was always very hopeful.

28
00:02:34,640 --> 00:02:39,120
It was about how we can use these technologies to advance the mission of Christ in the world.

29
00:02:40,080 --> 00:02:52,640
And that was a type of framework that was reinforced in me at a very early age of always thinking about technologies and emerging technologies and media as a tool that we can harness for good and for the mission of the church.

30
00:02:53,200 --> 00:03:01,760
There are obviously things that we need to be careful about and we need to use our discernment, but that was always the voice of the church from the very very beginning.

31
00:03:01,920 --> 00:03:13,440
We're talking about mid nineties and it continued until now with different things that the Popes and the Magisterium are, the Church Magisterium is teaching us on how to navigate these and how to be careful about it.

32
00:03:13,680 --> 00:03:15,440
So, but it's always been like that.

33
00:03:15,600 --> 00:03:21,040
It's always been in my heart the desire to leverage technology and media to evangelize.

34
00:03:21,760 --> 00:03:24,080
At the heart of it is, you know, the gospel is a story.

35
00:03:24,800 --> 00:03:29,520
It's the best story that can be told, the best story that has ever been told and can be told.

36
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:35,840
And I always wanted to use media and leverage media to tell that story to the world.

37
00:03:36,480 --> 00:03:46,640
Edmund, if you could share a little bit about your background and how your experience working in parish ministry has informed how you see technology being utilized and harnessed by the church.

38
00:03:48,080 --> 00:03:52,800
Yeah, I had ten years in parish ministry experience and I started out as a youth minister.

39
00:03:53,200 --> 00:04:00,960
So for me that meant having a pretty tight budget and having to do a lot to wear a lot of different hats.

40
00:04:01,680 --> 00:04:11,200
So, um, when you're in that situation and you're kind of, um, brave enough to experiment with different, um, tech or software tools and things like that.

41
00:04:11,840 --> 00:04:14,480
Um, that was definitely something I, I, I did.

42
00:04:14,880 --> 00:04:23,440
And so I think it shaped me by, um, on the one hand, like teaching me how to be, yeah, I guess scrappy and, and test things.

43
00:04:23,840 --> 00:04:31,520
But I think it also, um, showed me that, you know, the technology is like if I'm working on something the night before and then I'm in front of of people the next day.

44
00:04:31,760 --> 00:04:35,520
It's very quick to figure out what works and what doesn't with people.

45
00:04:36,240 --> 00:04:44,400
And so I think it helped me always place the person at the center of any technology or thing, you know, software or app that we were using.

46
00:04:45,120 --> 00:04:51,920
If it's not going to be helpful for ministry to other people, then, yeah, it just gets thrown out really quickly.

47
00:04:52,080 --> 00:04:59,600
So I think those two things, there's a lot of testing, experimenting, and then just seeing what worked and what didn't.

48
00:05:00,080 --> 00:05:06,400
And I think I still kind of have that mentality with the different tech and tools that I use today.

49
00:05:07,600 --> 00:05:14,720
What can you share about your experience in parish ministry and working with young adults, with adults and of course with younger people?

50
00:05:15,920 --> 00:05:30,400
Has technology become something central to almost every interaction or are there certain core interactions, core aspects of that ministry that even if we'd like to load it to technological efficiency or whatever, like it is centered around the people and that community?

51
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:35,520
Yeah, I mean the technology, I mean it's it's what is it?

52
00:05:35,680 --> 00:05:39,200
There's the Marshall McClugan quote about, you know, fish didn't discover water.

53
00:05:39,760 --> 00:05:57,360
Like, it's just such a part of our culture that even if you have teens or parishioners who aren't actively power users of technology, the whole culture is, is, has been impacted by technology and these tools and these, these inventions.

54
00:05:57,680 --> 00:05:59,840
So, yeah, it was a part of everything.

55
00:06:00,560 --> 00:06:20,400
And I'm sure both of you have heard so many stories of different parishes or ministries or even ministers who, um, kind of, you know, looked back lovingly on the days before all of these technological inventions, and then they kind of got left behind in the sense that it was really hard to communicate.

56
00:06:20,960 --> 00:06:24,480
It was hard to relate to people who this is part of their whole world.

57
00:06:25,040 --> 00:06:39,200
Um, you know, they might be with the church one or two hours a week and then all the other hours are spent in a culture and a society, just I mean, technology isn't just a part of that society, it is society.

58
00:06:40,080 --> 00:06:44,000
It's such a core part of the fabric of what we experience day to day.

59
00:06:45,120 --> 00:06:54,480
And then Edmundo, maybe to jump to you, the late Pope Benedict XVI spoke about the digital continent and the need to evangelize that continent.

60
00:06:54,880 --> 00:07:01,840
And Pope Francis, of course, spoke a lot about evangelizing online, using even the word, the term digital continent.

61
00:07:02,960 --> 00:07:04,400
Where do you think we are as a church?

62
00:07:05,120 --> 00:07:06,720
Are we pioneers on the digital continent?

63
00:07:07,040 --> 00:07:07,760
Are we settlers?

64
00:07:08,160 --> 00:07:09,040
Are we still tourists?

65
00:07:09,680 --> 00:07:10,640
What's your gauge on that?

66
00:07:11,520 --> 00:07:12,560
Yeah, it's a great question.

67
00:07:13,920 --> 00:07:27,440
I love that phrase from Pope Benedict because it helps us think in missionary terms, right, in our duty to carry a message, a message of hope, a message of life, right, into a place.

68
00:07:29,120 --> 00:07:30,960
Interestingly, the church has evolved.

69
00:07:31,680 --> 00:07:35,120
in the way that they talk about digital in general.

70
00:07:37,200 --> 00:07:41,120
With Pope Benedict, we got that phrase of the digital continent.

71
00:07:41,360 --> 00:07:44,800
Again, I think it's very helpful in many ways and I love it so much.

72
00:07:45,920 --> 00:08:05,280
But you see a change from then to Pope Francis and now with Pope Leo where the church understands it a little bit different and more along the lines of what Edmund was saying, they use the digital culture instead of digital content more now in church documents.

73
00:08:06,400 --> 00:08:08,560
So it kind of makes sense if you think about it.

74
00:08:09,200 --> 00:08:19,520
Pope Benedict and that generation and part of that generation too, even JP two, we were immigrants, right, coming into this digital continent, right?

75
00:08:19,680 --> 00:08:25,520
We were, we were, it wasn't something that we grew up with, but we had to learn how to be in it.

76
00:08:26,400 --> 00:08:29,760
And then you have millennials and younger, right, who are digital natives.

77
00:08:29,920 --> 00:08:34,800
And the church talks about these two groups of digital migrants and digital natives.

78
00:08:35,280 --> 00:08:39,280
But for digital natives, as Edmond was saying, this is, this is everything they know.

79
00:08:40,400 --> 00:08:46,480
And it's not like you can opt in or opt out, it's just part of the culture, it's part of where we are.

80
00:08:47,840 --> 00:09:00,480
So good things about the concept of a digital continent is the idea of being missionaries, of knowing that we are going into a foreign place to bring the gospel, the gospel message, a message of hope and life.

81
00:09:00,480 --> 00:09:02,720
in life that people need to hear.

82
00:09:03,360 --> 00:09:04,320
So that's super helpful.

83
00:09:04,560 --> 00:09:14,800
The digital culture language helps us understand that that is not something that we can avoid, but this is where we are immersed and this is what people are experiencing.

84
00:09:15,040 --> 00:09:18,800
It's part of their ecosystem, their ecology that they are a part of.

85
00:09:19,440 --> 00:09:31,280
So that's a more nuanced concept where regardless of your use of technology, you could be a heavy user or not in terms of digital tools, you're still part of these new digital c culture.

86
00:09:32,640 --> 00:09:36,640
Good news is that the gospel, the good news are truly good news.

87
00:09:37,600 --> 00:09:41,520
And this is what people desire and crave.

88
00:09:42,960 --> 00:09:44,080
I find it very interesting.

89
00:09:44,720 --> 00:09:53,200
You see all the data from this year in terms of the interest in the Catholic faith that has been registered in Google searches and so forth.

90
00:09:54,320 --> 00:09:58,720
It coincides with this new digital revolution connected to artificial intelligence.

91
00:09:59,360 --> 00:10:00,480
So we see two things.

92
00:10:01,040 --> 00:10:01,520
happening here.

93
00:10:01,760 --> 00:10:12,320
We see the increase in curiosity for faith, and then we see a world that is becoming more and more technologically complex and advanced.

94
00:10:12,880 --> 00:10:14,160
So we see the two things happening.

95
00:10:14,320 --> 00:10:16,480
I don't think it's a coincidence.

96
00:10:16,640 --> 00:10:17,840
I think it's connected.

97
00:10:18,320 --> 00:10:30,640
I think as people are more and more part of this digital culture, there is a desire for true encounter with God and true understanding of what human human, human, human, human, human, human, human.

98
00:10:33,120 --> 00:10:35,200
So I see the two of them very much connected.

99
00:10:35,440 --> 00:10:38,560
So for me this is a springtime of evangelization.

100
00:10:38,880 --> 00:10:54,160
This is the continuation of the Second Vatican Council and all those promises, you know, so fifty plus years after, you know, I think this is a time where we're going to see a lot of opportunity in the church because of these technological changes and what they bring to our culture and the questions that they pose.

101
00:10:55,200 --> 00:10:59,760
How do you see AI helping parishes serve people better?

102
00:11:00,800 --> 00:11:18,080
Well, I mean, I think part of the problem right now is that we're not seeing a lot of parishes using AI, but I think, you know, one of the really one of the strengths of AI is its ability to take a lot of information and synthesize it, organize it, things like that.

103
00:11:18,480 --> 00:11:30,880
So I think what the first thing I come to when I'm talking to someone who is not super familiar with AI but is in ministry is not what they would expect.

104
00:11:31,440 --> 00:11:37,680
They would expect often people go and try to use AI and they just type in like write my email for me or something like that.

105
00:11:38,160 --> 00:11:40,320
There are so many other tools that can take information.

106
00:11:40,560 --> 00:11:46,960
For example, notebook LM is one that I refer to a lot because you're able to upload a lot of information.

107
00:11:47,440 --> 00:11:55,840
You could, you know, ten church documents or a bunch of catechetical resources and then organize that information in interesting ways.

108
00:11:56,320 --> 00:12:00,080
So, especially moving into the age of AI.

109
00:12:01,280 --> 00:12:05,680
Information isn't the end all be all anymore, the valuable thing.

110
00:12:05,840 --> 00:12:07,200
It's more like the medieval times, right?

111
00:12:07,440 --> 00:12:13,200
It's how you combine information, like we said at the beginning, like to be useful to other people.

112
00:12:13,760 --> 00:12:21,440
So there's lots of ways that, yeah, AI makes quick organization and accuracy and things like that really useful.

113
00:12:22,640 --> 00:12:28,800
I mean, Mundo talks a lot about this really shifted my whole worldview on just ministry in general.

114
00:12:29,200 --> 00:12:32,400
Talks a lot about the difference between like mission and miss support and mission direct.

115
00:12:32,960 --> 00:12:52,480
And so I think there's a lot of opportunities in the mission support category for behind the scenes, analyzing data, getting more accurate information, and then also like personalizing how someone like the minister or someone being ministered to like access that information and data.

116
00:12:52,720 --> 00:13:00,720
So that's often the first thing I turn to because it's a lot easier to kind of wrap your head around is this like organizing processing information.

117
00:13:00,880 --> 00:13:03,040
information in more useful ways.

118
00:13:04,240 --> 00:13:08,880
Then Edmundo and Edmund, could you guys break down what is mission support and mission direct?

119
00:13:09,920 --> 00:13:11,040
Edmundo, go for it.

120
00:13:11,360 --> 00:13:13,120
Yeah, I'm happy to do that.

121
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:25,120
When I was working at the Archisee of Detroit, we came up with this system or the Archbishop came up with this system of or framework to think about the work that we do as a diocese, you know, as a local church.

122
00:13:26,400 --> 00:13:29,120
And we had this kind of division of labor, right?

123
00:13:29,360 --> 00:13:32,800
We have the mission support area and then we had the mission direct area.

124
00:13:33,760 --> 00:13:54,240
But mission support are all those, you know, for us we're departments, but functions, that's probably the best way to think about that, that are not doing mission directly, they're not impacting mission, but they are supporting mission or they're slowing down mission or making mission less effective.

125
00:13:54,400 --> 00:14:09,280
So that would be areas like finance, HR, technology that in an it, you know, they are not necessarily ministry, but if done well, you can really enhance ministry.

126
00:14:09,920 --> 00:14:14,800
So for mission support areas, what you want to look are for our efficiencies, right?

127
00:14:15,280 --> 00:14:24,240
How can we do this in a better way, faster way, so that we can free up resources, both monetary resources but also time to do more mission.

128
00:14:24,800 --> 00:14:30,480
And the mission direct area is everything that we do as a church that is the mission.

129
00:14:31,040 --> 00:14:42,480
So that would be Catholic schools and the ministry that we have and teaching children and teaching adults to the work that we do evangelization in the parishes, all the sacramental component of a parish.

130
00:14:42,720 --> 00:14:44,400
So that would be the mission direct.

131
00:14:45,200 --> 00:14:51,440
And in those, you really don't, you're not looking for efficiencies as much as effectiveness.

132
00:14:52,960 --> 00:15:00,640
Are we doing mission in a way that is accomplishing our goal, that people know and love the Lord, that they can encounter Jesus?

133
00:15:01,840 --> 00:15:08,560
So, you know, when we have this division, you know, there's efficiencies on one side, which is the most important part of it, and effectiveness on the other side of things.

134
00:15:09,360 --> 00:15:10,480
And both are connected.

135
00:15:10,720 --> 00:15:16,000
You need efficiency and effectiveness in both areas, but it just allows you to think differently about ministry.

136
00:15:16,640 --> 00:15:29,360
So when I think about artificial intelligence, it's like a no brainer to incorporate artificial intelligence in all of the mission support areas in a parish or in a diocese, because, you know, you want to free up resources.

137
00:15:29,520 --> 00:15:42,720
You want to have the ability to invest those resources in other things that are mission direct time and people, personnel and also, you know, monetary resources.

138
00:15:43,040 --> 00:15:44,320
So that's a no brainer.

139
00:15:44,640 --> 00:15:50,080
When it becomes more of a where people need a lot more wisdom and discernment is on the mission direct.

140
00:15:50,480 --> 00:15:52,320
How do you incorporate AI on the mission direct?

141
00:15:52,720 --> 00:16:02,400
And I think people that that's where there's a lot of concerns, I would say, from the local churches and, you know, priest pastors, lay Christian ministers and lay leaders.

142
00:16:03,280 --> 00:16:05,840
That's where we're trying to figure out how to do it.

143
00:16:06,960 --> 00:16:24,240
There are frameworks to think about that, and we can talk about it here that can move you from fear to confidence and from fear to hope in this age, but that's, I think, where the big conversation is happening right now and where people are concerned about it.

144
00:16:25,680 --> 00:16:44,240
And I think sad that it's more like you're an AI user, but you don't want to tell people because you're going to be seen as not putting people at the center and not having the human flourishing and the human person at the center.

145
00:16:44,560 --> 00:16:53,280
So you're kind of doing it but isolated instead of having learning communities where we're talking about how to do this in a way that advances the mission of the church.

146
00:16:53,440 --> 00:17:04,080
So I think that there's, you know, I need to find the exact code.

147
00:17:04,640 --> 00:17:15,520
But what stuck with me is that these are like talents, you know, when we talk about the parallel of talents that have been provided, you know, in God's providence have been available to us.

148
00:17:15,680 --> 00:17:17,600
And then we need to think about how to use information.

149
00:17:17,760 --> 00:17:25,200
So I encourage a greater conversation in pair settings and ISO settings for how do you incorporate AI on this mission direct?

150
00:17:25,760 --> 00:17:29,040
And mission support should be all about implementation, like best practices.

151
00:17:29,200 --> 00:17:30,320
I mean, companies are doing that.

152
00:17:30,320 --> 00:17:34,160
that like that's an easy win for a lot of parishes.

153
00:17:34,320 --> 00:17:38,400
And if you think of parishes, you're thinking of entities that have limited resources.

154
00:17:39,280 --> 00:17:49,360
You know, I love the definition of a pastor is someone who is overwhelmed, under-staffed and underfunded, you know, and that's a reality for most of the pastors.

155
00:17:49,520 --> 00:17:52,000
So incorporating AI and mission support should be a given.

156
00:17:52,560 --> 00:17:56,720
Now the fun conversation, the interesting conversation is how do we incorporate it into a mission?

157
00:17:57,600 --> 00:18:02,560
I have felt that feeling that a lot of people are using it it, but they just don't want to say it.

158
00:18:03,040 --> 00:18:07,840
And I think that with more honesty, that would be a beautiful thing, which is obviously what we want to do with Catholic Futurists.

159
00:18:08,480 --> 00:18:16,960
But Edmund, I think for a lot of our viewers and myself included, I don't know that much about what goes into organizing a parish.

160
00:18:17,600 --> 00:18:38,320
Everyone in the parish boundary is a member of that Catholic parish, meaning that everyone, Catholic or non Catholic, is part of that parish boundary, which means that that's the pastor's responsibility on behalf of the bishop and Jesus to evangelize, to disciple, to give the sacraments.

161
00:18:39,600 --> 00:18:43,440
So then the pastor goes, man, there are tons and tons of people in this parish boundary.

162
00:18:43,600 --> 00:18:52,560
Well, there are Catholics that show up for worship and they are part of the Christian community and hopefully they are also involved in this task of evangelization in the parish boundary.

163
00:18:52,960 --> 00:18:56,480
But again, like in today's day and age, these parishes are rather large.

164
00:18:56,800 --> 00:19:04,320
Even the smallest parishes that you could think of, it is hard for the pastor to be available to all of his people at all times.

165
00:19:04,640 --> 00:19:19,600
And so, you know, decades ago there was this, you know, more formalized movement of formalizing positions of volunteers who voluntarily get involved in the mission of that local church.

166
00:19:20,160 --> 00:19:33,520
And then we started seeing the like professionalizing of these roles as, you know, this laity got more and more educated as they get more training., they get more formalization around that, which is a great thing.

167
00:19:33,840 --> 00:19:43,760
So, you have in many diocese parishes that have some level of paid staff, although, um, admittedly, a lot of, I mean, it varies widely.

168
00:19:43,920 --> 00:19:46,800
Like there are parishes that have very few paid staff.

169
00:19:47,280 --> 00:20:10,240
So, um, you know, to avoid getting too much into like the canonical and theological aspects of it, like what goes into the parish is the pastor and either, you know, paid staff or a combination of paid staff and volunteers like cooperating with the laity to accomplish God's plan for our salvation in this local area.

170
00:20:10,560 --> 00:20:18,400
So there's kind of those three areas, you have the pastor, the laity, and then the geographic location, the mission territory.

171
00:20:19,760 --> 00:20:30,400
There are other things involved as well, but like focusing on that for our conversation here, like that's really the basic elements that we're talking about when we talk about this parish community.

172
00:20:32,480 --> 00:20:51,200
And I think most parishes don't spend enough time thinking about the people that don't show up, especially in the US church, if we're talking in particular about that, because for so many years we had this assumed cultural religiousness or assumed cultural Catholicness.

173
00:20:52,400 --> 00:20:58,560
And so a lot of people talk about people leaving the church and the Catholic Church in droves.

174
00:20:58,800 --> 00:21:14,960
And so there really is this, we've gone from there's a book from Chrysostom to Apostolic Mission, and I believe is the name of the book, but it's this idea that we no longer live in a civil, we live in a post Christian civilization and culture.

175
00:21:15,680 --> 00:21:27,200
So there really is this pressing need, as the Pope has recently talked about this, the past couple of Pope, like this need for this new evangelization, this re evangelization of our parish boundaries.

176
00:21:27,520 --> 00:21:30,320
And so like a Munas, I love what a Munas said, you know.

177
00:21:30,320 --> 00:21:37,440
You know, like we would be disappointed, we'd be embarrassed if we didn't use the tools that we need in youth ministry circles.

