00:44:30,960 --> 00:44:41,040
to think about how could we create these media and artificial intelligence literacy resources, courses, conversations for the parish now so that we are using not abusing technology.

344
00:44:41,840 --> 00:44:49,120
Now the question about the future, you know, in five, ten years, where do we want to be?

345
00:44:49,360 --> 00:44:54,880
So for me, I'm going to start with the most hopeful perspective because that's how I think about these things.

346
00:44:55,840 --> 00:45:04,160
Maybe this is my, you know, it was sharing that I started in 1994 when I was in school and that was my conversion and emerging technologies were happening.

347
00:45:04,320 --> 00:45:19,840
And I could see from there to now how we're able to share the gospel in such creative ways around the world where back in the day you needed to own a media company or work for a media company to put a message to the people.

348
00:45:20,160 --> 00:45:22,480
And now you can't do it without that.

349
00:45:23,040 --> 00:45:24,640
Anybody can publish a message.

350
00:45:25,360 --> 00:45:30,800
So I've seen that revolution from 1994 to now and what I see ahead.

351
00:45:31,520 --> 00:45:32,480
is maybe three things.

352
00:45:32,800 --> 00:45:52,720
One is AI is going to bring the cost down for a lot of the things that right now they're very expensive to do, and that's going to promote, I think, the leveraging media and creative tools to do beautiful things that communicate the gospel message in a very effective way.

353
00:45:53,040 --> 00:45:59,840
So I could see that being very important on how AI is going to change media production and communications for the good.

354
00:46:00,880 --> 00:46:33,680
I would love in five years to see all parishes leveraging AI for all the mission support things, from translations, active translations for other parishes that are multicultural to data analysis of the parish territories, Edwin was saying, and understanding trends and understanding opportunities to parish ministers that every year, the priests, the deacons, the late Piso ministers are getting better because they're using AI as a thought partner and a critical partner, and they're getting better at their homilies and their preaching and the catechesis and being better teachers.

355
00:46:34,240 --> 00:46:47,120
So I would love to see these AI tools agents helping the parish do things better, but never substituting the human interaction and what's uniquely human.

356
00:46:49,120 --> 00:47:11,760
So I would love in five years to be a church that is confident, a church that is not afraid, a church that understands what AI is and is not, and is able to leverage and harden its AI for good in a way that the Lord would be proud of us using these resources that he's allowed us to have at this time in this age, and that we're using it so that more people would come to know and love him.

357
00:47:12,400 --> 00:47:14,080
And Edmund, what about you?

358
00:47:14,720 --> 00:47:38,400
Yeah, I I man, I love doing this with Edmundo because it's nice, I can I can give maybe a different like a I don't know, maybe like a different take that I wouldn't I wouldn't have given like because I believe what Edmundo is saying, but I think the only thing I would add, I think I have a like a holy well, I hope it's holy, but I think I have a particular type of frustration with the church.

359
00:47:38,560 --> 00:47:57,200
So I think I want to paint a little bit more of a cautionary, like, like, picture that, you know, the internet, like I think we're going to see something very similar to what happened with the internet, where we're going to have similar patterns but just heightened, like intensified, and it's going to happen much faster and it's going to be more intense.

360
00:47:57,920 --> 00:48:00,480
So the internet democratized information.

361
00:48:00,560 --> 00:48:07,680
information, the internet in some ways started decentralizing leadership and authority, and these things happened really fast.

362
00:48:07,920 --> 00:48:30,240
You see this like boom in, you know, moms and, you know, state home parents and like college kids starting ministries and apostolates and these things and the parishes like the hearing of parishes during COVID that didn't have a list of any of their parishioners' e-mail addresses was really discouraging and and things like that, parishes like that.

363
00:48:30,800 --> 00:48:34,240
It was, it was a very negative effect, like it wasn't great.

364
00:48:35,200 --> 00:48:45,600
But I think what's really concerning is that as we move into this AI world, those types of mistakes are going to have more dire consequences and it's going to be quicker.

365
00:48:46,080 --> 00:49:00,880
So there's, uh, two, two ideas, like we're used to this idea of bell curves that, you know, more or less there's going to be a big group of people that have about average experience of things, whether it's resources or experiences, time or information.

366
00:49:01,760 --> 00:49:12,560
But with AI, we're having more of what's called these like power laws, where you have lots and lots of people that don't have much and then a small, small percentage that have a lot.

367
00:49:12,800 --> 00:49:24,080
So if you think back to like feudal society where you had a small, small one percent of the population had access to resources and riches and all of this, and then everyone else had a much, much different experience.

368
00:49:24,720 --> 00:49:30,720
Like I think in in my hopeful view of the future is that.

369
00:49:31,840 --> 00:49:52,320
Catholic leaders, parish ministers, pastors, don't see this as just a gimmicky tech fad, but see this as what it is, which is a revolution of society and the way we think and act and talk that forces us to be on the cutting edge of this.

370
00:49:52,800 --> 00:49:58,320
We have to be using these things or at least thinking about how to use these technologies.

371
00:50:00,320 --> 00:50:11,600
As Pope Leo said in that same address, he said, today we find ourselves in a new culture deeply characterized and formed by technology, and it's up to us to ensure that this culture remains human.

372
00:50:12,400 --> 00:50:24,160
So I think what I hope to see is that we understand that this is a really, really pressing time for the church to get involved in this technological revolution.

373
00:50:24,960 --> 00:50:26,480
Like we can't sit on the sidelines.

374
00:50:26,640 --> 00:50:30,400
And all the things Edmino said is like I believe one hundred percent that we are.

375
00:50:30,400 --> 00:50:39,280
We are given this huge opportunity to bring the church's wisdom, hope and confidence into these spaces, but we have to be involved in it.

376
00:50:39,600 --> 00:50:41,840
And we have to be involved in it yesterday.

377
00:50:42,480 --> 00:50:44,160
And it's exciting to be involved in it.

378
00:50:44,480 --> 00:50:45,440
And there is hope there.

379
00:50:46,640 --> 00:50:52,080
But I just really hope by 2030, we're seeing a new shift.

380
00:50:52,240 --> 00:50:55,280
Like we're seeing something we didn't see at the dawn of the Internet age.

381
00:50:55,440 --> 00:50:59,520
Like we're seeing a different response and reaction from Catholics everywhere.

382
00:51:02,400 --> 00:51:04,640
Edmund, thank you so much for that.

383
00:51:05,440 --> 00:51:08,480
Edmundo and Edmund, I'm very grateful for your time this morning.

384
00:51:08,640 --> 00:51:11,280
It has been a fascinating and just incredible conversation.

385
00:51:11,440 --> 00:51:17,360
I'm very grateful for you joining me on this and I hope to do it again soon, sometime.

386
00:51:18,480 --> 00:51:19,360
Oh, thank you, Ben.

387
00:51:19,840 --> 00:51:25,120
Maybe next time in Rome we should just go out and have a good conversation there.
